CSS Grid provides several powerful ways to center a div both horizontally and vertically. The simplest method uses place-items on the parent container:

```css
.parent {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;  /* Centers both horizontally and vertically */
}
```

This is equivalent to setting both align-items: center and justify-items: center. For more control:

```css
.parent {
    display: grid;
    align-items: center;  /* Vertical centering */
    justify-items: center;  /* Horizontal centering */
}
```

You can also use place-self on the child element:
```css
.child {
    place-self: center;  /* Centers within the grid cell */
}
```

Or use margin: auto on the child within a grid:
```css
.parent {
    display: grid;
}
.child {
    margin: auto;  /* Centers both axes */
}
```

CSS Grid is one of the most reliable methods for centering content, working well for both single elements and complex layouts.

